<https://w3id.org/gfo/base/Occurrent> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Occurrents have temporal parts and thus cannot be present at a time-boundary. Time belongs to them, because they happen in time and the time of the occurrent is built into it. The relation between an occurrent and a chronoid is determined by the projection relation.\nOccurrents are also called generalized processes in the GFO."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
